Z||000000||Seanh||10-24-2000||09:28 PM||||I was out working on the car, and I am trying to remove the rocker arm covers. Okay, I removed the dog bone, (and my suspisions were confirmed, there wa an awful lot of stress on it, and the engine droped a little, going to be a pain to hoist up) I then remove the bolt going to the front engine bracket. Now the Haynes manual tells me to loosen the lower bolt on the front engine bracket and remove the two upper bolts. I tried to remove the upper bolt tword the front, and it moves but, It also has the bracket to hold the two cords of wires going to the engine from the harness. The stupid bracket moves with the bolt. I tried holding the bracket as best I could being the only one here, and it bent a little and the bolt moved some, but my poor hand cant take holding it, that metal with as much force as it takes to move it, really kills your hand. What the heck should I do? There doesnt seem to be anyway to remove the two cords without first getting the bolt out. Also, another stupid question. The Haynes manual say that I need to remove the rear engine bracket at the exhaust manifold/head bolt location. Then I obviously have to also remove the bolt on the pass side on the engine holding that bracket and the alt bracket to the engine. I was wonding if this is safe? It seems like the engine might drop again? Is it normal for the engine to move like that when removing the dogbone? I know the bushings on it are shot, and need to be replaced. What should they be replaced with? Poly or rubber? I heard poly viberates more, but lasts longer. How much extra viberation is really transfered over the rubber bushings?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||8
Z||000001||StuGood||10-24-2000||10:14 PM||||Hmmm...it's probably the tach filter... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>J/K  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Usually, the motor shouldn't move very much, if you just unbolt the dogbone.  Between that, and the fact you say its bushings are already shot, chances are you'll need to replace the other mounts, too!  My <B>guess</B> is, you'll probably be happy with the stock rubber mounts, as opposed to the polys.<P>Haven't had a chance to look at the bracket situation yet, but I think you're talking about a little sheet metal one, that holds a roughly one-inch wire bundle from falling into the water pump pulley?  I'm working from memory, here.<P>In any case, please be gentle with this thing.  It takes patience, but it'll be worth it, if you take your time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.    <BR>    ||||reg||4
Z||000002||Seanh||10-24-2000||10:20 PM||||Tach filter? I figured it was that damn craked EGR tube.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Yep, thats the bracket Im talking about. I am still working with it. I hate that thing. I am probally going to need a new one before this project is over. Removing the heads, remind me why again I am doing this myself? Oh yeah, machanics here are stupid and rip offs.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
